WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.550
Film Courage: If we took a not so great or
a mediocre bland screenplay and we put it

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.750
on the same table and disassembled it we would
see not enough conflict and then we would

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.460
see and then we would see…well this is sort
of a bad term but emotional manipulation within

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.650
the story because you are saying that the
audience (these readers) they want these clues.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.390
They don’t want to be given the information

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.470
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.470
Media Arts: Right.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.820
Well as they say there are many more ways
of being sick than there are healthy.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.140
Film Courage: That’s true.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:35.989
Paul: So if we read a script that doesn’t
quite work for us then obviously the issues

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:38.320
are manifold.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:48.701
What kind of mistakes we make like with my
students…my students are brilliant and they

00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:55.690
hardly ever make mistakes but let’s just
say there is that outlier once in a while.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:08.480
There’s a tendency (in no particular order)
and I see it in feature films.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:16.840
There tends to be a misunderstanding in the
scheme that I’m talking about what does

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.330
it mean to have the end of the second act.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:27.720
It’s drummed into a lot of them that this
is the low point, the kind of all is lost

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:36.990
moment or everything is hopeless and everything
has to get really, really bad and it’s not

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:37.990
necessarily so.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.840
It depends on what your story actually is.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:49.830
The end of the second act is where the question
is answered but the third is it’s resolved

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:59.730
but preferably in a way that turns the story
into something deeper.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:08.099
Milos Foreman (when I studied with Milos Foreman
in the Columbia days) said that in a standard

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:14.849
classic feature with drama the audience usually
has two possibilities, the person gets it

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:15.849
or they don’t.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:19.180
So let’s say it’s hypothesis A or hypothesis
B.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:25.680
We hope for hypothesis A, we are afraid it’s
going to be hypothesis B. But the audience

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:33.730
isn’t satisfied if the third act is either
A or B. It’s got to be C. It’s got to

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:41.370
be something that transforms the way in which
we are aware of, a deeper meaning of the story.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:47.340
And that’s really what the function of the
third act is.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:55.879
We often feel like in the classic formulation
that I think most people go by when they are

00:02:55.879 --> 00:03:03.200
trying to write a script, the character wants…I
don’t have anything bad on my mind…but

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.299
let’s just say he wants the girl (it’s
easy).

00:03:06.299 --> 00:03:12.299
Let’s just say a bank and they want to make
money, they want to get their money back from

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:13.659
someone who stole it.

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:15.409
Let’s say that’s it.

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:21.200
They go through all of that, once they know
that they take these various actions to try

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.519
to get that objective.

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:31.209
Then it would be at the end of the second
act it doesn’t look like they will ever

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:33.150
going to get it.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.310
Okay.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.810
Oh it’s impossible.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:42.469
That’s a classical formulation.

00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:50.239
But there are other ways of seeing it that
can be very powerful and I try to encourage

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:54.909
my students to examine those when they are
looking at a story, when they are coming up

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:55.950
with it.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:01.340
What happens if they get it at the end of
the second act and it wasn’t what they thought

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.340
it was?

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.340
Or they paid a price to get it?

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.890
Or they paid a price on it?

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.599
Or the price was too big?

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:11.019
Or they did it but in the process damaged
someone so deeply that now they have to deal

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:13.819
with the consequences of what they did?

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:19.139
Those are ways of really turning a story and
examining the theme and making it powerful.

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:24.750
Like examples from the book and the analysis
that I do, in TOY STORY it’s about this

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.669
guy who wants to maintain his position at
the top, that’s what it’s about.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:34.580
And the question is A) He maintains it or
gets it back or B)

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.479
He never gets it back.

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:41.770
That’s how it’s presented but at the end
of the second act what happens?

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.030
Well it isn’t a question of that anymore.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:52.220
He gives up and he becomes selfless and then
the two team up.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.830
The question isn’t A or B, it becomes C.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:00.860
We are going to accept whatever the outcome
is but we are going to help each other.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:08.030
So you end up with this really uplifting ending
and you also (when you do this) when you open

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:15.811
yourself up to that possibility, you can also
reverse the tension in that way.

00:05:15.811 --> 00:05:21.449
The typical feeling is I love this character
(let’s look at the tennis court), I love

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:24.639
that character, they are in deep trouble and
I want them to win.

00:05:24.639 --> 00:05:31.349
But there are times when the character wants
something and you don’t want them to get

00:05:31.349 --> 00:05:34.139
and that tension is perfectly valid.

00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:40.280
And we see it in the classic like THE APARTMENT
where the main character wants to climb his

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:41.480
way to the top.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:46.300
But that’s not what we ultimately want for
him.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:55.110
What we want is for him to be a true human
being who is not degrading himself constantly

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.240
to get to the top.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.810
We want him to be a full human being who gets
true love.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:02.820
That’s what we want.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.129
That’s not what he wants.

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:07.500
That’s not what he is pursuing.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:14.490
So at the end of the second act, he gets everything
he could possible want and he doesn’t like

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:15.490
it.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:19.060
And now we explore the movie from a different
point of view.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.830
You take the premise and you flip it.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.759
And that’s something where I have to work
with my students because there is an automatic

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:30.400
tendency to go with A or B.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:39.070
An example of a movie that I feel could have
used a rethinking of it was (I’m probably

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:45.400
going to forget the name of it)…THE REVENANT
that came out a few years ago.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.890
At the root of it, it’s a very simple revenge
story.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:56.760
This guy…left me to perish and lied about
it and I’m going to just get that guy.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.030
So what is the tension?

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.110
He’s either going to get him or he isn’t.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.169
And along the way we have these sub-scenes
and they are not all against that guy.

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:05.289
None of them are.

00:07:05.289 --> 00:07:12.810
They are all about overcoming the elements
and being helped by somebody and the winter

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:17.509
weather and the river that would have hurt
him in 30 seconds but this is Hollywood so

00:07:17.509 --> 00:07:19.210
it all worked out okay.

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:20.410
So it gets all the way to the end

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:26.460
Now you are in a position (the filmmakers
managed to put themselves in a position) that

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.560
it was going to be predictable.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:33.840
The final fight is either they are going to
get the guy or he isn’t going to get the

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:34.840
guy.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.409
And he’s probably going to get the guy.

00:07:37.409 --> 00:07:43.220
So inevitably in this kind of movie where
they have limited options as storytellers

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:45.169
it’s going to come down to come down to
a fight.

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:50.409
And the fight is going to have a moment where
the bad guy almost gets him!

00:07:50.409 --> 00:07:52.819
Maybe there’s a sharp object and “Aahhhhh!!!!”

00:07:52.819 --> 00:07:58.360
But somehow the good guy finds just enough
reserve of energy, where it comes from I don’t

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.360
know.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.860
But somehow throw him off and get the upper
hand.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.039
And they perish horribly.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:10.740
In this case he doesn’t get him, but he
does pretty much the same thing to him that

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:12.310
was done to him.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:18.979
But look at that story I mean what are the
other options if that story comes to me and

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:20.740
I’m looking at it.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:24.949
Well the main character is a guy who wants
revenge.

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:26.880
Is that even the most interesting character?

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.210
This is what I would do, turn this around.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.870
What are the other characters here?

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.769
Well there’s the “bad guy.”

00:08:32.769 --> 00:08:35.519
You can always…the bad guys don’t think
they’re bad guys.

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:37.260
They think they are doing it right.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.110
They are just living with the times and going
with the flow.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.550
So this guy leaves him to perish.

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:43.640
Why does he leave him?

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:48.370
Well he thinks he’s going to be a drag on
the group but they’re all going to perish.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:52.310
That’s not a bad thing, that’s a difficult
question.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:57.760
Now the have painted this character as an
evil guy but there’s another character there,

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:00.930
an army officer that are leading them.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:06.839
What if the men are his responsibility and
he knows for certain that if we keep this

00:09:06.839 --> 00:09:10.920
guy, my men could perish.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.910
Now that’s an interesting problem.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.670
So he does something questionable, he leaves
him to perish (or he thinks he’s hurt).

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:24.910
And then he finds out that the guy is alive
and that to me is more interesting dilemma

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:29.650
and the third act could be the confrontation
between them or it could be something more

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:30.650
interesting.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.720
It’s not just going to be A or B. It’s
going to be how do I redeem myself?

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.760
How do I get this person to understand I didn’t
do it?

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:38.760
What are the issues?

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.590
It becomes a lot richer of a story.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:48.089
He could probably still get a go in the river
(the frozen river) and you could probably

00:09:48.089 --> 00:09:49.780
still have that stuff.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:54.230
But you are not locked into something that
is just A or B.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:10:01.260
So that’s what I see when you are talking
about a script that doesn’t quite work.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:07.329
A lot of good things in it but you are kind
of left with…uh…okay…let’s go home.

00:10:07.329 --> 00:10:11.950
It doesn’t lead me away.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:20.980
The thing in terms of story development when
I work with students there is this old Zen

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:28.390
saying, so if we can be a little Zen here
for a short bit of time but there is a saying

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:32.519
to a beginning student that the rivers are
rivers and the mountains are mountains.

00:10:32.519 --> 00:10:36.070
But to an intermediate student the rivers
are no longer rivers and the mountains are

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:38.180
no longer mountains.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.240
To an advanced student though…guess what?

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.170
The rivers are rivers again and the mountains
are mountains.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:52.070
You can probably Google that and I’m sure
you will find it under Zen sayings about mountains.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:56.730
But to a beginning screenwriting student,
you want to write a script?

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.560
Just start writing a script?

00:10:58.560 --> 00:10:59.560
Write the scenes.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.579
Okay I’ve got this…okay I’m going to
start writing.

00:11:01.579 --> 00:11:07.260
And if you put a bunch of scenes together,
you will have a script, you will have a story.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:09.360
An intermediate student learns no, no, no.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:10.740
You have to plan the story first.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.350
You’ve got to know what is the story.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:18.490
How do you know what scenes you are going
to have unless you have the story and it tells

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.810
you and I’ve run into this.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:27.000
When I read an unsolicited script very often
I start with a lot of notes which are pertaining

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.920
to the moments (the scenes).

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.690
Then I realize a few pages in wait a minute?

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:34.550
I don’t even know if the story needs those
scenes?

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:36.670
I’ve got to figure out what the story is
because the story needs those scenes.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.110
I’ve got to figure out what the story is
because then I’m wasting my time writing

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:42.770
detailed notes on scenes that should be thrown
out because you don’t need them or they

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:44.800
are not optimal for the story.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.019
So that’s the intermediate students.

00:11:47.019 --> 00:11:54.779
But when you get to the advance level you
realize (going back to constructivist psychology)

00:11:54.779 --> 00:11:59.509
audiences don’t actually see stories at
all.

00:11:59.509 --> 00:12:00.509
They don’t.

00:12:00.509 --> 00:12:02.329
What do they see?

00:12:02.329 --> 00:12:04.800
They see scenes?

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.980
They see scenes and in the process of watching
the scenes they construct a story in their

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.820
minds, they put the scenes together and they
understand that there is a story.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:15.810
But the story is not materially present ever.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.840
It’s always just constructed in our heads.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:23.460
And therefore because all you see in our scenes,
when you have a premise the way you want to

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:30.260
develop it is the amount of really cool scenes
in it.

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:33.350
I’ll sometimes see students where here’s
a story and I have to stick with it.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:37.260
But the way you’ve developed the story,
you’re not allowing for any really interesting

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:38.260
scenes.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:40.019
I’ve seen those scenes, the ones in THE
REVENANT.

00:12:40.019 --> 00:12:41.019
I’ve seen it in variations.

00:12:41.019 --> 00:12:43.420
And I’ve seen that fight before.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:47.649
But maybe you could turn the story in a way
that “Yes, that’s going to be a really

00:12:47.649 --> 00:12:48.649
cool scene.

00:12:48.649 --> 00:12:50.420
And what about that one?”

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:54.610
Don’t you realize that if you withhold the
information from the character now you have

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:58.079
a scene that has a double meaning to it.

00:12:58.079 --> 00:13:08.730
There is a scene in a script that I consulted
on and did a rewrite on where the father finds

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:15.310
the daughter with a lover she shouldn’t
be with and it was like “YOU!

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:17.502
HOW COULD YOU DO THAT!”

00:13:17.502 --> 00:13:18.502
And it was like “No father…I hate you.”

00:13:18.502 --> 00:13:21.830
And it was exactly the kind of scene you’d
see anytime, anywhere.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:28.699
But the way I re-did it was the lovers are
together and they see the father coming and

00:13:28.699 --> 00:13:36.199
the daughter hides and the father sees the
guy and he’s really in a tough mood, he’s

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:40.069
struggling with his career and he sits down
with the guy (with the daughter right there)

00:13:40.069 --> 00:13:45.050
and he’s talking about his relationship
with his daughter and how he doesn’t understand

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:49.399
her and how he really wants…everything is
really rich now!

00:13:49.399 --> 00:13:53.319
Because we know that the daughter is hiding
there and that’s the really cool scene,

00:13:53.319 --> 00:13:54.319
let’s do that.

00:13:54.319 --> 00:13:59.401
Let’s put that scene there rather than one
we’ve seen a million times and it can all

00:13:59.401 --> 00:14:02.710
come down to how you choose to develop it.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:07.440
So scripts that don’t quite work I’ve
seen that pattern with the third act.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:16.709
I’ve seen choices made in terms of developing
that don’t maximize the possibilities that

00:14:16.709 --> 00:14:20.800
are present there.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:25.690
There is the question of (which we have not
talked about) the dialogue.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.100
Of what dialogue is…(to be continued).

